WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:00.959
- Hey guys.

00:00:02.083 --> 00:00:03.083
Thank you for having me.

00:00:03.083 --> 00:00:05.083
This is a beautiful room.

00:00:05.083 --> 00:00:06.500
I'm very excited to be here.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.083
And very honestly, I do want
to get into Q&amp;A pretty quickly,

00:00:10.083 --> 00:00:13.917
and I don't want to
bore you with my life story

00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:14.750
or things of that nature.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.500
But here's where I will go.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.041
In doing some of my
homework before I got here

00:00:18.041 --> 00:00:20.917
and some of the
talking that we did beforehand,

00:00:20.917 --> 00:00:25.375
what I really feel
excitement about is even more so

00:00:25.375 --> 00:00:29.208
than in the U.S. market,
where I've been able to

00:00:29.208 --> 00:00:31.458
what I call day trade attention.

00:00:31.458 --> 00:00:35.792
Which is, very simply, no
matter what you do in this room,

00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:38.166
you've got to
communicate your value, right?

00:00:38.166 --> 00:00:39.750
No matter what
you do in this room,

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.375
whether you're in B to B,
whether you're in B to C,

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.750
whatever you sell,
whatever you do,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.208
if you're running for office,
if you're trying

00:00:46.208 --> 00:00:48.041
to raise money for a non-profit,

00:00:48.041 --> 00:00:51.542
if you're trying to sell
sneakers, if you're a lawyer,

00:00:51.542 --> 00:00:53.792
if you're trying to raise your
profile to get a promotion,

00:00:53.792 --> 00:00:54.792
or get a different job.

00:00:54.792 --> 00:00:58.834
Regardless of what
you do, what your real job

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:03.250
at the macro is to find
where people's attention is

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:04.125
and then try to figure out how

00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:06.583
to communicate your value prop.

00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:10.166
When I see a market
like this that has decided,

00:01:10.166 --> 00:01:11.041
and this is holistically,

00:01:11.041 --> 00:01:12.291
this is not every
individual person,

00:01:12.291 --> 00:01:15.708
but has decided things like
television or outdoor media,

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:20.000
or even more
specifically, direct mail,

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:22.583
become the
preference points in a world

00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:24.750
where Facebook has 

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.250
such underpriced
attention potential,

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.917
it just gets me very excited.

00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:33.417
The reason we opened a
VaynerMedia London office is

00:01:33.417 --> 00:01:36.917
when we carefully looked
at the cost of Facebook ads

00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:39.750
in western Europe,
they were stunning to us

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:42.041
on how much
better of a deal it was

00:01:42.041 --> 00:01:44.208
than the U.S. penetration.

00:01:44.208 --> 00:01:45.959
What's interesting
about that, and some of you

00:01:45.959 --> 00:01:48.458
that follow me know this,
and usually when I talk,

00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:50.500
I talk in U.S. marketing terms,

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.083
we believe that
Facebook is disproportionately

00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:56.875
the best deal in the marketplace
today in that market.

00:01:56.875 --> 00:01:59.375
So, as you can imagine, it
created enormous excitement

00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:02.458
for me about the
potential in markets

00:02:02.458 --> 00:02:04.708
like Belgium, U.K., and
other places of that nature.

00:02:04.708 --> 00:02:09.333
For me, very simply, this
speech is 

00:02:09.333 --> 00:02:10.959
one minute of content,

00:02:10.959 --> 00:02:14.417
which is, everything
I've done for 20 years

00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:16.375
has been the same exact move.

00:02:16.375 --> 00:02:18.083
What does everybody
spend their time and money

00:02:18.083 --> 00:02:19.834
and energy on?

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:21.708
Is that worth it?

00:02:21.708 --> 00:02:24.792
And then what can
I do that is emerging,

00:02:24.792 --> 00:02:27.959
that is underpriced, because
people don't have reports

00:02:27.959 --> 00:02:30.542
to justify it at
Corporate America?

00:02:30.542 --> 00:02:32.875
Or, on the entrepreneurial
level, they're scared

00:02:32.875 --> 00:02:36.375
to do it because
they're fake entrepreneurs.

00:02:36.375 --> 00:02:40.041
For me, that has been the
tried-and-true real history

00:02:40.041 --> 00:02:40.875
of my career.

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:43.125
It started all the way
back when I got involved

00:02:43.125 --> 00:02:45.208
in my dad's liquor store.

00:02:46.542 --> 00:02:49.583
It is literally only
20 years ago, 

00:02:49.583 --> 00:02:52.083
now almost 21 years ago,

00:02:52.083 --> 00:02:53.792
which is not that long of
a time if you really think

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:57.125
about it, that when we
launched WineLibrary.com,

00:02:57.125 --> 00:03:00.792
everybody told me and
my dad that it was a fad.

00:03:00.792 --> 00:03:03.583
Forget about is SnapChat a fad.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:06.667
Forget about if
Instagram is a fad.

00:03:06.667 --> 00:03:10.875
The entire internet was
a fad if you were really

00:03:10.875 --> 00:03:15.125
a business person
in 1995, 1996, 1997.

00:03:15.125 --> 00:03:16.208
It's fascinating to think that.

00:03:16.208 --> 00:03:19.166
It's the same way
that many of you in here

00:03:19.166 --> 00:03:23.125
probably don't realize if
you're not studying the space,

00:03:23.125 --> 00:03:25.208
that we're probably
only 20 or 30 years away

00:03:25.208 --> 00:03:29.125
from virtual reality
making the internet obsolete

00:03:29.125 --> 00:03:31.542
and all of us just
living in contact lenses

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:34.000
and in a VR world,
and the real world

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.750
and the internet
world become arbitraged out

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.792
because what will
happen is our attention

00:03:38.792 --> 00:03:41.834
will now be in a
new virtual reality.

00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:44.125
For me, this is
how my brain works.

00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:48.625
To me, I'm not a digitalist
over a traditionalist.

00:03:48.625 --> 00:03:51.000
For example, in
the American market,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.375
one of the things I'm really
pushing our clients towards

00:03:53.375 --> 00:03:55.375
is actually radio.

00:03:55.375 --> 00:03:57.333
There's a
platform called IHeartRadio

00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:00.375
that I believe that the ads on
that platform are underpriced

00:04:00.375 --> 00:04:04.125
for the attention because people
listen to that programming.

00:04:04.125 --> 00:04:07.959
When the disc jockey
jumps in and reads a live ad,

00:04:07.959 --> 00:04:09.792
I actually think
that's a good deal.

00:04:09.792 --> 00:04:11.166
That confuses a
lot of my friends

00:04:11.166 --> 00:04:12.917
that think I'm a digitalist.

00:04:12.917 --> 00:04:15.083
In America, if you
were to launch a product,

00:04:15.083 --> 00:04:16.959
and you had
$20 million to launch it,

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.542
the first thing I would
do is buy a Super Bowl ad.

00:04:19.542 --> 00:04:21.708
Because in America,
every single person

00:04:21.708 --> 00:04:24.750
watches the
Super Bowl commercials.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.458
And so, this is how I trade.

00:04:26.458 --> 00:04:28.583
This is how I think.

00:04:28.583 --> 00:04:31.208
And the truth is,
much like what's happening

00:04:31.208 --> 00:04:33.291
with your agency,
I've always thought

00:04:33.291 --> 00:04:35.792
it was a huge
advantage to not be in America,

00:04:35.792 --> 00:04:38.041
not be in Silicon Valley.

00:04:38.041 --> 00:04:41.792
In a world where so much
of what we do is just human,

00:04:41.792 --> 00:04:44.041
not American or
European, or Asian.

00:04:44.041 --> 00:04:47.375
It's just human psychology,
not everything maps.

00:04:47.375 --> 00:04:48.959
Obviously Americans
are more comfortable

00:04:48.959 --> 00:04:50.875
being outspoken and in front.

00:04:50.875 --> 00:04:52.750
Some countries
are more reserved.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.208
Some countries have
political instability

00:04:55.208 --> 00:04:56.291
that makes them scared to share.

00:04:56.291 --> 00:04:58.500
There's different
variables across the world.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:00.583
But when I look at this market,

00:05:00.583 --> 00:05:02.708
it's interesting
to me and exciting.

00:05:02.708 --> 00:05:05.041
Because the truth is,
you actually get to watch

00:05:05.041 --> 00:05:07.542
the future play out
in a different market

00:05:07.542 --> 00:05:10.667
and then all you have to do is
interpret it to your market.

00:05:10.667 --> 00:05:12.959
And then all you
have to do is be patient.

00:05:12.959 --> 00:05:14.959
See, the real reality here is,

00:05:14.959 --> 00:05:18.208
the far majority of
upside sits in this room

00:05:18.208 --> 00:05:20.708
with the individuals
here that map similar

00:05:20.708 --> 00:05:23.375
to my overall thesis,
which is they're patient,

00:05:23.375 --> 00:05:25.917
and they're waiting
for it to come to them.

00:05:25.917 --> 00:05:30.375
But they're good enough in
staying alive to get there.

00:05:30.375 --> 00:05:34.208
The backbone of my success
that I don't talk a lot about

00:05:34.208 --> 00:05:37.000
is the fact that
I'm so good at sales

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.208
that I can scrounge by.

00:05:39.208 --> 00:05:40.792
And that's what
the VaynerMedia was.

00:05:40.792 --> 00:05:45.125
In 2009, '10, '11, when nobody
wanted to buy social media,

00:05:45.125 --> 00:05:48.708
I was good enough to
just get by, stay alive,

00:05:48.708 --> 00:05:50.708
stay alive, stay alive.

00:05:50.708 --> 00:05:52.792
But, I was the loudest and

00:05:52.792 --> 00:05:54.959
proudest about
believing in this.

00:05:54.959 --> 00:05:56.625
I had to be right.

00:05:56.625 --> 00:05:59.125
Because again, in 2009,
'10, '11, most people thought

00:05:59.125 --> 00:06:00.875
these sites would go away.

00:06:00.875 --> 00:06:02.708
This was coming on
the back of Myspace--

00:06:02.708 --> 00:06:04.792
(phone ringing) Make
sure you pick that up.

00:06:04.792 --> 00:06:06.333
(audience laughter)

00:06:06.333 --> 00:06:09.041
This was on the back of
Myspace in the U.S. market

00:06:09.041 --> 00:06:12.083
and people were still debating
if every site would have

00:06:12.083 --> 00:06:13.375
a one or two year thing.

00:06:13.375 --> 00:06:15.083
Everybody was very
emotional about investing

00:06:15.083 --> 00:06:16.291
in new platforms.

00:06:16.291 --> 00:06:18.000
I had to stay alive.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.417
I was able to stay alive, and
then the market came to me.

00:06:21.417 --> 00:06:23.708
Patience is such a big deal.

00:06:23.708 --> 00:06:26.125
The thing that concerns me,
and that I'm curious about

00:06:26.125 --> 00:06:29.083
in this room, is of the
300-plus people in here,

00:06:29.083 --> 00:06:31.041
how many people
actually sell something

00:06:31.041 --> 00:06:33.542
they fully believe 
in in their heart,

00:06:33.542 --> 00:06:36.166
versus they sell it
because it's what they're doing,

00:06:36.166 --> 00:06:37.583
or it's what
they've committed to,

00:06:37.583 --> 00:06:40.208
and it's where
their money is tied into?

00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:43.792
To me, this is a very
important part of being

00:06:43.792 --> 00:06:47.875
in the agency,
brand, startup world.

00:06:47.875 --> 00:06:51.959
If you do not fundamentally
believe in what you're selling,

00:06:51.959 --> 00:06:55.125
and I mean really, not
how you posture to each other,

00:06:55.125 --> 00:06:57.834
but how do you talk to yourself
in your own brain at night.

00:06:57.834 --> 00:06:59.750
If you do not,
you're vulnerable.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.166
You're very vulnerable
for many different reasons.

00:07:02.166 --> 00:07:04.041
But my belief is now looking

00:07:04.041 --> 00:07:05.542
at the global
advertising market.

00:07:05.542 --> 00:07:06.375
It's amazing to me.

00:07:06.375 --> 00:07:09.125
When I go to Cannes every year,

00:07:09.125 --> 00:07:10.708
I'll sit on a panel like this.

00:07:10.708 --> 00:07:13.834
Usually with somebody from
WPP and somebody from Omnicom,

00:07:13.834 --> 00:07:17.208
and a big brand, and usually
it's a gang up session,

00:07:17.208 --> 00:07:19.083
three against me.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:22.208
Which is usually a blowout

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:24.041
because I can
slaughter the three of them.

00:07:24.041 --> 00:07:25.375
It's not enough.

00:07:27.458 --> 00:07:28.417
They bash me.

00:07:28.417 --> 00:07:30.667
Tried-and-true reporting,
bullshit reporting,

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:35.000
Nielsens, Millward Brown
horseshit, fake math.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:38.625
And then we'll end,
then we'll leave.

00:07:38.625 --> 00:07:41.542
And then,
at 3:00 in the morning, 

00:07:41.542 --> 00:07:44.750
after 11 glasses of rosé,

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.417
they'll see me at
the Carlton Hotel.

00:07:46.417 --> 00:07:48.458
They'll come up to
me and they'll say,

00:07:48.458 --> 00:07:51.417
"Psst. You're right."

00:07:51.417 --> 00:07:54.041
I find this to be the
single most fascinating thing

00:07:54.041 --> 00:07:57.000
about the advertising
and marketing landscape.

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.375
In a world where
Facebook is disproportionately

00:07:59.375 --> 00:08:02.417
the best direct-to-consumer
advertising,

00:08:02.417 --> 00:08:04.166
the fact that Wine Library,
my family business,

00:08:04.166 --> 00:08:07.208
has moved all of its direct
mail business into Facebook.

00:08:07.208 --> 00:08:09.291
The math isn't even close.

00:08:09.291 --> 00:08:11.834
There's people in here
doing direct mail marketing

00:08:11.834 --> 00:08:14.083
who have made a
decision that that works

00:08:14.083 --> 00:08:15.834
without spending
the same amount of money

00:08:15.834 --> 00:08:18.166
on Facebook to
see if that works.

00:08:18.166 --> 00:08:21.041
We are living in a
headline-reading society.

00:08:21.041 --> 00:08:23.333
Everybody has opinions.

00:08:23.333 --> 00:08:25.083
Everybody has
opinions about everything,

00:08:25.083 --> 00:08:27.917
but they're not
actual practitioners.

00:08:27.917 --> 00:08:30.250
You can't have an
opinion on Snapchat

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:32.875
or Snapchat ads if
you've never used it.

00:08:32.875 --> 00:08:35.750
You can't have a
smart opinion to me

00:08:35.750 --> 00:08:40.041
if you've run
$1,000 in Facebook ads

00:08:40.041 --> 00:08:41.834
and have made
your entire decision

00:08:41.834 --> 00:08:46.667
on the plethora of opportunites
on the platform on $1,000.

00:08:46.667 --> 00:08:48.041
Clients tell me
all the time,

00:08:48.041 --> 00:08:50.291
"Gary, we know
television commercials work,

00:08:50.291 --> 00:08:51.625
"we don't if know
if Facebook works."

00:08:51.625 --> 00:08:53.792
I go, "No shit,
you spent $8 million on

00:08:53.792 --> 00:08:55.708
"a television
commercial campaign

00:08:55.708 --> 00:08:57.291
"in production and distribution,

00:08:57.291 --> 00:09:00.375
"and you spent
$100,000 testing Facebook."

00:09:00.375 --> 00:09:02.417
Of course it's
not going to work.

00:09:02.417 --> 00:09:04.875
And so we live
in this environment

00:09:04.875 --> 00:09:07.291
where people go and play safe.

00:09:07.291 --> 00:09:11.083
You make decisions, so many
of you in here make decisions

00:09:11.083 --> 00:09:14.166
on what's best for your
career in the short-term,

00:09:14.166 --> 00:09:16.959
not what actually sells shit.

00:09:16.959 --> 00:09:19.542
And to me, that's where
all the opportunity sits.

00:09:19.542 --> 00:09:21.667
That's where the action is,
that's the white space.

00:09:21.667 --> 00:09:24.875
That's why in America,
in the fashion industry,

00:09:24.875 --> 00:09:27.333
every brand is in trouble
because there are thousands

00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.792
of little brands doing Facebook
and Instagram influencers,

00:09:30.792 --> 00:09:32.750
and they're chopping
away at the big brands.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.166
Ralph Lauren's
biggest problem isn't Prada,

00:09:36.166 --> 00:09:39.750
it's 41 women and men
startups that are doing

00:09:39.750 --> 00:09:43.208
from zero to three
million in revenue in one year

00:09:43.208 --> 00:09:46.083
because they're marketing
in underpriced channels

00:09:46.083 --> 00:09:49.083
while Ralph Lauren and
Prada are buying full page ads

00:09:49.083 --> 00:09:52.500
in fuckin' Vogue
that nobody's seeing.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.542
This is happening,
this is happening every day.

00:09:55.542 --> 00:09:57.458
And here's the
funniest part, I know

00:09:57.458 --> 00:10:01.000
that everybody in this
room knows it's happening.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.583
I just don't know if it actually
is in your best interest

00:10:03.583 --> 00:10:06.667
for it be happening
in the short-term.

00:10:06.667 --> 00:10:09.166
That to me is the bet,
that to me is the strategy.

00:10:09.166 --> 00:10:12.625
That is the only thing I care
about pushing in this room.

00:10:12.625 --> 00:10:14.667
It's why I want to go
to Q&amp;A sooner, I'd rather go

00:10:14.667 --> 00:10:17.542
into details 'cause
the thesis is very simple.

00:10:17.542 --> 00:10:20.750
I can say this
again five more times

00:10:20.750 --> 00:10:22.125
and get to the Q&amp;A part,

00:10:22.125 --> 00:10:23.834
but it will not change,
it will be the same thing.

00:10:23.834 --> 00:10:25.667
I will give you
four different analogies

00:10:25.667 --> 00:10:27.166
for the same fucking thing.

00:10:27.166 --> 00:10:31.667
Attention has a price,
everything still works,

00:10:31.667 --> 00:10:34.708
the problem is most
things are overpriced.

00:10:34.708 --> 00:10:37.500
For example, in
America and the globe,

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:41.000
programmatic buying for digital
has completely taken over.

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:44.291
Why? It's profitable
for media agencies.

00:10:44.291 --> 00:10:46.667
But it's not good for
brands and businesses.

00:10:46.667 --> 00:10:49.375
Who the hell here
goes to a desktop computer

00:10:49.375 --> 00:10:51.959
and goes to blah
blah blah dot com

00:10:51.959 --> 00:10:54.083
and goes to the
right side of the website

00:10:54.083 --> 00:10:57.500
below the fold and
clicks a banner ad?

00:10:57.500 --> 00:10:59.500
Nobody.

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:01.625
So in a world where
I put enormous pressure

00:11:01.625 --> 00:11:04.000
on my clients to move
money away from television

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:08.375
because nobody,
nobody in the U.S. market

00:11:08.375 --> 00:11:10.500
and nobody in the global
market, if you look at the math,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.208
is really consuming a
television commercial.

00:11:14.208 --> 00:11:16.667
Every single time
you're in a position

00:11:16.667 --> 00:11:18.083
where a television
commercial is about

00:11:18.083 --> 00:11:21.208
to give you
something, you grab this

00:11:21.208 --> 00:11:24.250
and you check your
work, you talk about

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.208
what you just watched
'cause you're watching...

00:11:26.208 --> 00:11:28.625
I love when my
competitors on stage say,

00:11:28.625 --> 00:11:30.792
"Gary, television
ratings are through the roof."

00:11:30.792 --> 00:11:34.375
I'm like, "Yes,
television shows."

00:11:34.375 --> 00:11:37.208
Everybody likes to sneak
